31	https://sites.usc.edu/new-student-health-requirements/general-faqs/	2026-11-13 18:44:54.714245-08	200	General FAQs – Health Requirements for Students Skip to content USC Website Toggle search interface Search Search Site Close search interface Menu Toggle extended navigation Health Requirements for Students Start Here Toggle "Start Here" dropdown Read me first! MySHR Quickstart Guide Consent for Treatment Prevention Education Modules Webinars and Resources For Parents Toggle "For Parents" dropdown For Parents (Zhongwen Edition) 致家长（中文版） Required Immunizations Toggle "Required Immunizations" dropdown Uploading Vaccination Records FAQs (Multi-lingual) Signed Vaccine Records (Multi-lingual) Special Clearances: TB Screening (International Students Only) Special Clearances: Clinical Health Professions Special Clearances: DC Cohort Planning Your Care Toggle "Planning Your Care" dropdown Top Tips Students Should Know Medication management or chronic conditions Using Services / Locations Understanding Costs Toggle "Understanding Costs" dropdown Student Health Fee (SHF) Health Insurance Requirement Insurance Waiver Requirements Important Notice: AB 594 Glossary of Health Insurance Terms Contact Toggle "Contact" dropdown Questions? Sign Up for a Chat General FAQs More Menu Toggle extended navigation Home General FAQs General FAQs Waivers Can I waive the Student Health Fee (SHF)? No. All students enrolled in 6 or more credits; all international students; and all students enrolled in health professions degree programs are required to pay the student health fee; they then have access to campus health services. This appears on each semester’s student financial account. Can I waive the Student Health Insurance Plan (SHIP)? Yes. If you have a plan that meets the minimum requirements of the university, you may submit documentation to waive the Student Health Insurance Plan (SHIP/Aetna). ONLINE and IN PERSON students who have their own plan must submit a waiver by the Friday of the third week of classes, each fall semester, in order to reverse automatic enrollment in SHIP/Aetna. Immunizations I am in a graduate-level hybrid program (meets online and in person); do I need to meet the immunization requirements? Yes. If you are in an in-person degree program, you are required to meet the immunization requirements. If you think you may have been assaulted while incapacitated/unconscious, we encourage that you still contact an advocate to explore your medical concerns and forensic options as soon as possible after the incident. For more information regarding drug and alcohol facilitated sexual assaults contact an RSVP advocate. I am in a graduate-level online degree program (meets online only); do I need to meet the immunization requirements? No. Only students who are in in-person classes are required to meet the immunization requirements. Copays Are there co-pays for visits to medical or counseling appointments in Student Health? No. There are no co-pays or other out-of-pocket visit fees, or billing to insurance, for appointment visits with providers in Student Health. Insurance does apply for lab and radiology services, immunizations, and care-related equipment (crutches and boots). Note: Provider services outside of Student Health (for example, Psychiatry and Behavioral Health Services, the private practice on the 5th floor of Engemann), prescriptions and pharmacy services, etc., may require co-pays when using your insurance. Patient Privacy Are my records kept confidential ? Yes. Your records are private and confidential in compliance with ethical and legal standards including FERPA and HIPAA. Immunizations/Insurance Deadline is Jan 29, 2027 Complete your immunizations by the first day of classes (January 11, 2027). If you cannot complete requirements before coming to campus, you are required to come to the immunizations fair (“Immu-Fair”) during the first week of classes. If your immunization records are incomplete by Jan 29, 2027 , a registration hold will be applied to your student account. Review and make a decision on health insurance by this same deadline date. If you choose to use your own insurance (must meet minimum criteria) and waive the SHIP/Aetna plan, you must do so by this date, or the SHIP/Aetna plan and the relevant premiums will be non-reversible on your student account. Immunization Documentation Examples Please note that records must show your name and date of birth in order to be accepted. Screenshots for health portal records are accepted. For records in languages other than English, translations into English must be provided. Example of a PRC immunization card/booklet Translated Immunizations Example of a lab report Please note that lab reports should include the following information to verify: a. Patient’s Full Name b. Patient’s Date of Birth c. Name of Test Performed d. Date of Test e. Test Result Numeric Values (cannot just say “positive” or “negative”) f. Test Reference Ranges g. Ordering Provider Name CONTACT Location and Hours (213) 740-9355 (WELL) [email protected] Tell Us What You Think SOCIAL Instagram OUR SITES • Sex and Health • Counseling and Mental Health • Confidential Advocates Resources and Education • Staying Well • Athletic Medicine • Insurance (SHIP) ABOUT US Mission, Values, and Vision Annual Report 2025-2026 LEADERS & PROVIDERS Leadership Meet the Providers JOIN OUR TEAM Employment Opportunities Training for College Mental Health Professionals Student Jobs INTRANET Intranet (Log-in required) POLICIES / INFORMATION Patient Privacy and Confidentiality Patient Rights and Responsibilities Medical Policies and Forms FEES Fees and Deadlines List For Online Degree Program Students INTERPRETER SERVICES Available upon request UPDATES Staying Well at USC Patient Materials HEALTHY CAMPUS AND WELLBEING PARTNERS Newsletter Wellbeing Collective ONE Safety USC Guides for Faculty Student Health Advisory Committee Meet the Health Promotion Strategy Team University of Southern California Privacy Notice Notice of Non-Discrimination Digital Accessibility close menu Search Search Site Start Here Toggle "Start Here" submenu Read me first! MySHR Quickstart Guide Consent for Treatment Prevention Education Modules Webinars and Resources For Parents Required Immunizations Toggle "Required Immunizations" submenu Uploading Vaccination Records FAQs (Multi-lingual) Signed Vaccine Records (Multi-lingual) Special Clearances: TB Screening (International Students Only) Special Clearances: Clinical Health Professions Special Clearances: DC Cohort Planning Your Care Toggle "Planning Your Care" submenu Top Tips Students Should Know Medication management or chronic conditions Using Services / Locations Understanding Costs Toggle "Understanding Costs" submenu Student Health Fee (SHF) Health Insurance Requirement Insurance Waiver Requirements Important Notice: AB 594 Glossary of Health Insurance Terms Contact Toggle "Contact" submenu Questions? Sign Up for a Chat General FAQs	134e354dd91dcfa046e937d08068cd871fe56b1ed2acd6782cd6bb185027db86	\N
32	https://studentbasicneeds.usc.edu/programs-and-services/economic-support/	2026-11-13 18:44:54.714245-08	200	Economic Support - Student Basic Needs Skip to main content Go to usc.edu homepage Student Basic Needs Home About Show submenu for About Accessibility Information Programs & Services Show submenu for Programs & Services Food Support Housing Support Economic Support Tommy’s Closet Resource Navigation & Partnerships Get Involved & Volunteer Show submenu for Get Involved & Volunteer Donate Contact More… Show More Menu Search for: Home Programs & Services Economic Support Economic Support Published January 20, 2026 Last Updated February 13, 2026, 11:20 am At USC Student Basic Needs, we support students experiencing economic insecurity with care, confidentiality, and compassion. Economic insecurity refers to inconsistent access to the financial resources, services, and supports needed to maintain a basic standard of living while pursuing a degree. We provide individualized support and connect students to critical resources, including: Supplemental Funding Awards – financial support to help cover essential expenses such as food, rent/housing, healthcare, or childcare during times of unexpected need Financial Navigation Consultations – one-on-one sessions to help students create personalized budgets, plan around financial aid disbursements, and leverage campus and community resources effectively If you are currently experiencing financial hardship—or are unsure whether your situation qualifies—please complete a contact form . Submit contact form Our economic support services are intended to complement students’ existing financial resources by providing short-term assistance during times of unexpected hardship. While not designed to replace primary funding sources such as financial aid, employment, or family support, these programs help students bridge financial gaps, cover urgent needs, and access tools that build long-term stability. By offering timely support, we aim to reduce stress, promote well-being, and allow students to remain focused on their academic and personal goals. Student Basic Needs Supplemental Funding The Student Basic Needs Supplemental Funding is a one-time funding opportunity designed to help currently enrolled students who are experiencing sudden financial distress due to unforeseen circumstances. These awards are available for students experiencing financial difficulties that jeopardize their success or continued enrollment at USC and who have exhausted all other resources. Examples may include, but are not limited to, housing costs, food costs, or unforeseen dental/medical costs. Examples of how awards can be used: Housing/rent expenses; Food expenses; Unforeseen medical/dental expenses; Childcare expenses Examples of how awards cannot be used: Transportation expenses (e.g. gas); Vehicle repairs/expenses (e.g. car parts); Books, supplies, or technology expenses; Tuition & fees Eligibility Student must be enrolled at least half time, and in the term (i.e. Fall, Spring, Summer) funds are being requested Have sudden financial distress due to unforeseen circumstances Have a $0 bill balance with the university Receive approval from Financial Aid. Student Basic Needs will connect with the Financial Aid Office on your behalf Must be able to supply any additional information requested such as bills, receipts, or other documents Application Process Once you have connected with USC Financial Aid (if applicable), and have met the above eligibility requirements, please complete the Student Basic Needs Supplemental Funding Award application via USC Scholarship Universe . Students must log in using the USC Undergraduate Applicant or USC Graduate Applicant option. Submit application Fall & Spring Application Cycle The Supplemental Funding Award is a limited, need-based resource that opens around the sixth week of each semester. The application remains open for a two-week window, during which students are encouraged to apply as early as possible. After the application period closes, the review and processing phase begins, which typically takes up to four weeks. During this time, the Student Basic Needs team and campus partners carefully review each application to ensure eligibility and consistent distribution of available funds. Applicants will be notified of their funding decision once this process is complete. Resources Explore financial resources available to support students with funding access, money management, and navigating economic challenges during their academic journey. USC Financial Aid Emergency Information : immediate resources for those who have been impacted by the Los Angeles area fires Help and Contact Phone: (213) 740-4444, 9:00 a.m. – 5:00 p.m. Pacific Time (M-F) Online Assistance and Email Inquiries Special Circumstances and Appeals Undergraduate Special Circumstances and Appeals Graduate & Professionals Special Circumstances and Appeals Special Populations International Students ROTC & Veterans Benefits Federal Work-Study Graduate Students Undergraduate Students Scholarship Universe USC Scholarship Universe is an online tool that helps currently enrolled students identify scholarship opportunities that align with their academic background, interests, and financial circumstances. By completing a brief profile, students can access both USC-affiliated and external scholarships in one centralized platform. Scholarship Universe is designed to simplify the search process and connect students to funding opportunities that may help offset educational and living-related expenses. Visit Scholarship Universe USC Graduate Student Government Graduate Student Government (GSG) offers several funding programs designed to support graduate students facing unexpected financial challenges, caregiving responsibilities, accessibility needs, and professional development or academic travel costs. These funds help reduce financial barriers that may impact students’ academic progress and well-being. Available funding opportunities include: GSG Emergency Fund Supports graduate students experiencing unexpected financial emergencies. GSG Caretaker Subsidy Fund Provides assistance to graduate students who are primary caretakers to help offset caregiving-related expenses. GSG Empowerment Award Supports professional development needs such as conferences, certifications, or academic enrichment opportunities. GSG Travel Award Assists graduate students with costs related to academic or conference travel. GSG Neuropsychological Evaluation (NPE) Fund Helps offset the cost of neuropsychological evaluations required for diagnostic clarity and access to academic accommodations. Students should review eligibility requirements and application timelines directly through GSG. USC Credit Union USC Credit Union offers free financial wellness resources to support students in building money management skills. Services include workshops, online courses, one-on-one coaching, and educational events available year-round. Visit USC Credit Union Food Support Trojan Food Pantry CalFresh Emergency Meal Swipe Housing Support Economic Support Tommy’s Closet Resource Navigation & Partnerships Student Basic Needs 1025 W 34th Street King Hall KOH STE 103 Los Angeles, CA 90089 USC Student Life, University of Southern California Email: basicneeds@usc.edu Follow us @uscbasicneeds Site Navigation Home About Accessibility Information Programs & Services Food Support Housing Support Economic Support Tommy’s Closet Resource Navigation & Partnerships Get Involved & Volunteer Donate Contact Donate to USC Student Basic Needs There are many ways to support USC students experiencing food, housing, and economic insecurity. Make a donation University of Southern California Content managed by Student Basic Needs Built using the USC Gateway 2018 WordPress Theme | Website issues? Contact USC Provost IT Privacy Notice | Notice of Non-discrimination | Digital Accessibility | Sitemap Translate	