vity Institute Bridge Institute Center for Computational Language Sciences Center for Ecological and Evolutionary Dynamics Center for Music, Brain and Society Center for New Technologies in Drug Discovery and Development Center for Peptide and Protein Engineering Center for the Changing Family Center for Synthetic Living Systems Convergent Science Institute in Cancer Dana and David Dornsife Cognitive Neuroimaging Center New Microbes For New Beneficial Natural Products Psychology Services Center USC Sea Grant PHYSICAL SCIENCES Agilent Center of Excellence in Biomolecular Characterization Center for Applied Mathematical Sciences Center for Quantum Information and Science Technology Center of Excellence in Molecular Characterization Center of Excellence in NanoBiophysics Core Center of Excellence in Nano Imaging Loker Hydrocarbon Research Institute Statewide California Earthquake Center The Plastics Problem The Urban Critical Zone Explore All Browse all of our departments, programs, centers, and institutes Continuing Student Scholarships MENU Home Dornsife Continuing Student Scholarships Application Scholarship Descriptions Recommendations FAQ Research, Travel, and Internship Grants Other Scholarships Other Scholarship Applications The following scholarships do not use the general online USC Dornsife Continuing Student Scholarship application. Each has its own unique application process and deadline. Please note that this is not an exhaustive list of all scholarships available at USC. Scholarship names with stars (asterisks) in the list below have applications currently available. Click on the name of the scholarship for further details, including the application deadline and link to the application form. Tuition scholarships for current/continuing Dornsife students **Trojan League of Los Angeles, Joseph Patrick Allen Endowed Scholarship** Established to honor the life of Joseph Patrick Allen, former Dean of Admission and Financial Aid and Vice Provost for Enrollment at USC. Funds shall be used for tuition only, and shall not be allocated for room and board, research expenses, or supplies. Recipients of the 2027-2028 award must be full-time students enrolled in the Dana and David Dornsife College of Letters, Arts and Sciences at USC beginning their junior year in the 2027-2028 academic year Must have entered the university with a high school GPA of at least 3.67 and have a current minimum cumulative USC GPA of 3.0 Undergraduates should complete a minimum of 12 units per semester, plan to graduate in four years, and maintain a minimum 3.0 cumulative GPA Must not be related to a current member of the Trojan League of Los Angeles Board or Scholarship Committee Must have demonstrated merit and financial need, academic strengths, leadership skills, and campus and community involvement Preference given to permanent residents of Southern California A letter of recommendation from a USC Dornsife faculty member is required (emailed to scholarships@dornsife.usc.edu ) Application deadline: Tues. February 16, 2027 Download the application for the 2027-2028 Trojan League of Los Angeles Scholarship. The Trojan League of Los Angeles, Joseph Patrick Allen Endowed Scholarship is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Dean Joan Metcalf Schaefer Merit Scholarship The Dean Joan Metcalf Schaefer Merit Scholarships in USC Dornsife reward students each year who follow in Dean Joan’s footsteps of scholarship, leadership and service. “It is the ultimate honor to have a living legacy named in one’s lifetime,” she once said. An interview is required for this scholarship. Scholarship semifinalists will be notified during the week of March 30 and must be available to interview with the selection committee on Saturday, April 11, 2026. Recipients of the 2026-2027 awards must be full-time undergraduate students majoring in the USC Dana and David Dornsife College of Letters, Arts and Sciences for the 2026-2027 academic year Junior or senior class standing in the 2026-2027 academic year Minimum 3.5 grade cumulative point average A letter of recommendation from a USC Dornsife faculty member is required The application deadline for the 2026-2027 Dean Joan Metcalf Schaefer Merit Scholarship has passed. Please visit this webpage in January 2027 for the application for the 2027-2028 Dean Joan Metcalf Schaefer Merit Scholarship. The Dean Joan Metcalf Schaefer Merit Scholarships are administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. J. Wesley Robb Endowed Scholarship in Human Values Established in 1987, this scholarship honors Professor Emeritus J. Wesley Robb and his lifelong service to the University of Southern California. The award was established by administrators, faculty, and former students of Professor Robb. Over 9,000 students took his “Human Values” course or one of his seminars in medical ethics, philosophy of religion, or contemporary ethical issues. Students are chosen for their demonstration of significant moral leadership in the campus community, evidence of a commitment to human values, and a deep concern for others. Recipients of the 2026-2027 J. Wesley Robb Scholarship must be majoring in the USC Dornsife College of Letters, Arts and Sciences during the 2026-2027 academic year Senior class standing in the 2026-2027 academic year Minimum 3.5 cumulative grade point average A letter of recommendation from a USC faculty member is required The application deadline for the 2026-2027 J. Wesley Robb Scholarship has passed. Please visit this webpage in January 2027 for the application for the 2027-2028 J. Wesley Robb Scholarship. The J. Wesley Robb Endowed Scholarship in Human Values is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Michael B. Preston Scholarship in Urban Politics and Civil Rights Established to honor the life of Michael B. Preston, a valued USC professor and administrator who left behind a great legacy of teaching, scholarship, and service. Recipients of the award must be full-time undergraduate students in the 2026-2027 academic year with a major in Political Science who are studying urban politics or civil rights Minimum 3.0 cumulative grade point average Students graduating in Fall 2026 are eligible to apply A letter of recommendation from a USC faculty member is required The application deadline for the 2026-2027 Michael B. Preston Scholarship has passed. Please visit this webpage in January 2027 for the application for the 2027-2028 Michael B. Preston Scholarship. The Michael B. Preston Scholarship in Urban Politics and Civil Rights is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Lesher Family Scholarship Established in honor of Professor Alison Dundes Renteln, this award will provide assistance for promising undergraduate students. Recipients of the award must be full-time undergraduate students in the 2026-2027 academic year with a major in Political Science; International Relations; International Relations and the Global Economy; Law, History and Culture; Philosophy, Politics and Economics; Philosophy, Politics and Law; or Political Economy Demonstrated interest in human rights, political freedom, international law, leadership, or public policy Minimum 3.0 cumulative grade point average At the time of application, applicants must have already completed at least two courses in Political Science, with at least one of these courses being an upper-division Political Science course The application deadline for the 2026-2027 Lesher Family Scholarship has passed. Please visit this webpage in January 2027 for the application for the 2027-2028 Lesher Family Scholarship. The Lesher Family Scholarship is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Mark Greenberg Fellowship in Poetry Established by Mark’s sister Elizabeth to reward outstanding achievement in poetry. Junior class standing in the 2026-2027 academic year Creative Writing major with an emphasis in Poetry 3.0 cumulative grade point average The application deadline for the Greenberg Fellowship in Poetry has passed. Please visit this webpage in January 2027 for the application for the 2027 Greenberg Fellowship in Poetry. The Mark Greenberg Fellowship in Poetry is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Brian Phillip Rakusin Neuroscience Scholarship Award Established in memory of Brian Phillip Rakusin, USC’s first Neuroscience major. Successful candidates must demonstrate outstanding achievements and aspirations in the field of Neuroscience. Neuroscience major Cumulative GPA of 3.2 or higher Recipients must have sophomore or junior standing and must remain on campus for a year following receipt of the award Must demonstrate excellence in both coursework and research Award honorees will be decided by the Executive Committee of the Undergraduate Neuroscience Major Program. Learn more about the 2026-2027 Brian Phillip Rakusin Scholarship . For questions about the application, please contact Susan Wong at wongsusa@usc.edu . The application deadline for the 2026-2027 Brian Phillip Rakusin Scholarship has passed. The Brian Phillip Rakusin Neuroscience Scholarship Award is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. University-wide tuition scholarships for current/continuing USC students Skull and Dagger Scholarships The Skull and Dagger Foundation is pleased to announce that six $5,000 scholarships will be awarded to continuing USC students for the 2026-2027 academic year. The scholarships will be awarded to students (graduate or undergraduate) who have demonstrated significant campus and/or community leadership. The Skull and Dagger Society was founded in 1913 and is the oldest honor society at USC. Its members have been selected because of their service, dedication, and performance on behalf of the University. Two of the scholarships are named in memory of Dr. Kenneth Owler Smith, a professor of Journalism who served as an officer of the Society from 1983 to 1988, and Dr. Harrison M. Kurtz, a professor of Biological Sciences who served as an officer of the Society from 1989 to 2001. The Barden Centennial Scholarship is dedicated in memory and honor of Bella and Don Barden, whose daughter Alana Marshall Barden is a member of the 2013 Centennial Class of the Skull and Dagger Society. It is supported by the Bella and Don Barden Foundation, which strives to promote growth, leadership, and entrepreneurship in youth and pledges commitment to the community. The William I. Griffith Scholarship is named in memory of Bill Griffith, a member of the Class of 1946, who served as editor of the Daily Trojan . The fifth scholarship is named in memory of Dr. Robert and Marjorie LaBriola, established by their son, Rocky LaBriola, a former officer of the Society. The sixth scholarship is name in memory of Darlene Hard, a member of the Society, who was a world championship and Hall of Fame tennis player, as well as a 45-year employee of the University and Student Publications office. If you are selected as a semifinalist, you must be available for an in-person interview on Wednesday, April 15, 2026, 6:00-8:00pm PT. If you have any questions about the application, please contact jerrypapazian@gmail.com . The application deadline for the 2026-2027 Skull and Dagger Scholarships has passed. Please visit this webpage in February 2027 for the application link for the 2027-2028 Skull and Dagger Scholarships. Skull and Dagger Scholarships are administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. **Half Century Trojans Scholarship** The Half Century Trojans Scholarship is open to continuing USC undergraduate students who are direct descendants (grandchild or great-grandchild) of (1) a person who received an undergraduate degree from USC 50 years ago or more, or (2) a person who received an undergraduate degree from a college or university 50 years ago or more and later received a graduate degree from USC. Selection criteria include academic achievement, potential for leadership, and community service. An interview is required for scholarship finalists. Finalists will be contacted by the Half Century Trojans Scholarship Committee. Application deadline: Tues. February 16, 2027 Learn more about the Half Century Trojans Scholarship. Half Century Trojan Scholarships are administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Jason Baringer Memorial Scholarship The Jason Baringer Memorial Scholarship honors the memory of Jason Baringer, who attended the University of Southern California until January 2001 and passed from complications of Muscular Dystrophy. This scholarship provides financial assistance to undergraduate or graduate students with physical disabilities, specifically mobility, who are registered with the USC Office of Student Accessibility Services. Positive consideration is given to students with a major within the USC Dornsife College of Letters, Arts and Sciences. The application deadline for the 2026-2027 Jason Baringer Memorial Scholarship has passed. Please visit this webpage in January 2027 for the application link for the 2027-2028 Jason Baringer Memorial Scholarship. The Baringer Memorial Scholarship is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Research, internship, and test prep grants for current USC students Student Opportunities for Academic Research (SOAR) and Student Undergraduate Research Fund (SURF) The SOAR program offers Dornsife students an opportunity to apply for funding up to $1,000 to participate in Maymester/Julymester programs or as a research assistant in a Dornsife faculty mentor’s project during the fall and/or spring semesters. SURF funding up to $3,000 is awarded for Problems without Passports Programs (PWP) or research with Dornsife faculty during the summer. Declared major in Dornsife Full-time enrollment 3.0 minimum GPA Participation in a Maymester/Julymester or PWP program, or approved research work with Dornsife faculty Visit the SOAR webpage and/or the SURF webpage for more information. Direct questions regarding SOAR to soar@dornsife.usc.edu and questions regarding SURF to surf@dornsife.usc.edu . SOAR and SURF awards are administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. USC Dornsife Pre-Health Test Prep Scholarship The USC Dornsife Pre-Health Test Prep Scholarship is available to assist students in need of financial support to enroll in a test preparation program for the MCAT, DAT, PCAT, GRE, or other applicable standardized exam for entry into a health professional school. Scholarship awards vary in amount from $300 – $2,500. Students may receive full or partial scholarships. Any undergraduate major at USC Plans to pursue a career in healthcare Junior or senior class standing Full-time enrollment at USC during the 2025-2026 academic year Completion of most or all of the required prerequisite core classes for the selected pre-health track Plans to sit for a standardized exam within the next three to six months Minimum USC cumulative GPA of 3.3 Minimum USC science GPA of 3.3 The application deadline for the 2026 USC Dornsife Pre-Health Test Prep Scholarship has passed. Learn more about the USC Dornsife Pre-Health Test Prep Scholarship . For questions or application assistance, contact the Dornsife Pre-Health Advising Office at (213) 740-4844 or prehealthscholarship@dornsife.usc.edu . The USC Dornsife Pre-Health Test Prep Scholarship is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Bhutani Summer Research and Travel Award Provides funding for undergraduate Classics majors to undertake a research project with a faculty member abroad or in the United States during Summer 2026. Classics major 3.5 minimum cumulative grade point average The application deadline for the 2026 Bhutani Award has passed. Please visit this webpage in January 2027 for the application for the 2027 Bhutani Award. The Bhutani Summer Research and Travel Award is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. KCM Undergraduate Grant Program Provides grants to undergraduate students for participation in co-curricular programs and internships offered through the USC Environmental Studies Program and the USC Wrigley Institute for Environmental Studies. Environmental Studies or Environmental Science and Health major -OR- Environmental Studies minor with a Dornsife major Completion of at least one full-time semester at USC -OR- full-time enrollment in Spring 2025 3.25 minimum cumulative GPA and major GPA The application deadline for the 2026 KCM Undergraduate Grant Program has passed. Please visit this webpage in January 2027 for the application for the 2027 KCM Undergraduate Grant. The KCM Undergraduate Grant Program is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Cathy and George Huber Endowed Scholarship Provides grants to undergraduate students for participation in co-curricular programs offered through the USC Environmental Studies Program and the USC Wrigley Institute for Environmental Studies. Preference is given to students participating in programs held on Catalina Island, although awarding is not limited to those programs alone. Environmental Studies or Environmental Science and Health major Completion of at least one full-time semester at USC -OR- full-time enrollment in Spring 2025 3.25 minimum cumulative GPA and major GPA The application deadline for the 2026 Huber Endowed Scholarship has passed. Please visit this webpage in January 2027 for the application for the 2027 Huber Endowed Scholarship. The Cathy and George Huber Endowed Scholarship is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Roberta Persinger Foulke Endowment Fellowship Established by USC Alumna Roberta Persinger Foulke (BA/MA History 1936). Provides assistance for students who demonstrate the ability or desire to further interests in the field of history or historical studies. Two award categories exist: Support can be used for tuition only. You can apply for the Merit Award by completing the general USC Dornsife Continuing Student Scholarship application and submitting an additional essay for the Foulke Merit Award. Research and Travel Grants will be given to undergraduate students with the best proposals to use funds for scholarly research, travel, or other departmentally-approved programs. Funds will be applied to items such as travel expenses, conference fees, or research materials and can be used in the summer months. Foulke Research and Travel Grant applicants must: Be a USC undergraduate student with a major or minor in a History department course of study Have either completed at least one semester at USC (12 units or more) or be enrolled full-time (12 units or more) in Spring 2026 Have a minimum GPA of 3.5 in your History courses Have a minimum cumulative GPA of 3.0 The application deadline for the Summer 2026 Foulke Research and Travel Grant has passed. Please visit this webpage in January 2027 for the application for the Summer 2027 Foulke Research and Travel Grant. The Roberta Persinger Foulke Endowment Fellowship is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Carrie D. and C.J. Walker Endowed Scholarship Established in the early 1900’s. Provides assistance for undergraduate Religion majors and minors. Two award categories exist: Merit Awards will be given to undergraduate Religion majors or minors who demonstrate academic excellence. The support can be used for tuition only. You can apply for the Merit Award by completing the general USC Dornsife Continuing Student Scholarship application and submitting an additional essay for the Walker Scholarship. Research and Travel Grants will be given to undergraduate Dornsife majors with the best proposals to use funds for scholarly research, travel, or other departmentally-approved programs with the Religion department. Funds will be applied to items such as travel expenses, conference fees, tuition for off-campus language study, or research materials and can be used in the summer months. Walker Research and Travel Grant applicants must: Be a USC undergraduate student with a major or minor in Religion Have either completed at least one semester at USC (12 units or more) or be enrolled full-time (12 units or more) in Spring 2026 Have a minimum cumulative GPA of 3.0 and a minimum GPA of 3.5 in Religion coursework The application deadline for the Summer 2026 Carrie D. and C.J. Walker Endowed Scholarship has passed. Please visit this webpage in January 2027 for the application link for the Summer 2027 Carrie D. and C.J. Walker Endowed Scholarship. The Carrie D. and C.J. Walker Endowed Scholarship is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Salz Pollak Fellowship for Undergraduates in Comparative Literature Established by USC Almuna June Pollak (B.A. ’49, M.A. ’51, Ph.D. ’61), the Salz Pollak Fellowship for Undergraduates in Comparative Literature will provide research and travel grants for undergraduate students who are majoring or minoring in Comparative Literature. Research and travel grants will be awarded to undergraduate students majoring or minoring in a Comparative Literature course of study who seek funds for scholarly research, travel, or other departmentally-approved programs, including courses of language study related to the Comparative Literature degree and Maymester or other summer programs with a comparatist emphasis. Students may apply for funding to assist in travel to archives or libraries, as part of the preparation of an honors thesis or other substantial research project, to acquire language skills for scholarly work, to complete a summer program essential to their Comparative Literature course or study, or to facilitate conference presentations. Awards will be made in the form of summer stipends. The application deadline for the Salz Pollak Fellowship for Undergraduates in Comparative Literature has passed. Please visit this webpage in January 2027 for the application for the Summer 2027 Salz Pollak Fellowship for Undergraduates in Comparative Literature. The Salz Pollak Fellowship for Undergraduates in Comparative Literature is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Salz Pollak Fellowship for Undergraduates in the English Department Established by USC Alumna June Pollak (B.A. ’49, M.A. ’51, Ph.D. ’61 English), the Salz Pollak Fellowship for Undergraduates in the English Department provides assistance for students who demonstrate the ability or desire to further the interests in the field of English or English studies. Research and Travel Grants will be given to undergraduate students majoring or minoring in an English Department course of study who offer the best proposals to use funds for scholarly research, travel, or other departmentally approved programs, in particular those whose research focuses on women or the family. Funds will be applied to items such as travel expenses, conference fees, or research materials and can be used in the summer months. The application deadline for the Summer 2026 Salz Pollak Fellowship for Undergraduates in the English Department has passed. Please visit this webpage in January 2027 for the application link for the Summer 2027 Salz Pollak Fellowship for Undergraduates in the English Department. The Salz Pollak Fellowship for Undergraduates in the English Department is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Helen Leslie Milligan Turner Summer Fellowship Established in 2020 in honor of Helen Leslie Milligan Turner who graduated from USC in 1957 with a Bachelor of Science degree in human biology (formerly kinesiology). Mrs. Milligan Turner enjoyed her time at USC and wanted to give back to students who are interested in research in human biology. Provides a limited number of fellowships for undergraduate summer projects. Each undergraduate student funded by the program will receive a stipend in the amount of $3000 to support reaching the goals of the project, which can be in any academic sub-field of Human and Evolutionary Biology. The project can have a research, clinical, educational or community focus; should require a period of not less than ten weeks at 10 hours per week during the summer months; and should be sponsored by one full-time USC faculty. Student eligibility requirements Students must: Be majoring in Human Biology and working under the supervision of a faculty advisor housed in either Human and Evolutionary Biology (HEB) or any other section of Biological Sciences; however, preference will be given to HBIO students supervised by HBIO faculty. Enrolled full-time (12 units per semester) at USC for the entire academic year (at least the Fall semester following the summer research project). 5th year progressive degree students may be eligible if the student’s bachelor degree has not yet been completed and would be in progress during the Fall semester following the summer research project. Graduating seniors are not eligible. Not have received other concurrent funding for this project (a recipient of the Provost Fellowship (PURF), Rose Hills, etc. will not be contemporaneously considered). Have a cumulative GPA of 3.0 or better The application deadline for the Summer 2025 Helen Leslie Milligan Turner Fellowship has passed. Please visit the Human Biology webpage for further information. The Helen Leslie Milligan Turner Summer Fellowship is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Center for Advanced Genocide Research Fellowships The USC Dornsife Center for Advanced Genocide Research offers fellowships for students from any academic discipline to conduct original research drawing on the unique resources in Holocaust and Genocide Studies at USC including the Holocaust and Genocide Studies book collection, the Special Collections at USC Libraries, and the Visual History Archive. These fellowships provide a unique opportunity to work with exceptional archival resources, engage deeply with history and human experiences, and pursue questions tied to students’ own interests. Projects need not focus solely on genocide but should draw meaningfully on USC’s Holocaust and Genocide Studies resources and the testimonies they engage. Award decisions for the fellowships will be based on the originality of the research proposal. The Beth and Arthur Lev Student Research Fellowship provides support for undergraduate students for a one-semester residency during Spring 2027. The Charles E. Scheidt Undergraduate Research Fellowship provides support for a two-semester residency during Spring 2027 and Fall 2027. Visit the page on CAGR Research Fellowships for further details, and visit the CAGR website for more information about the Center. For questions regarding the application process, or for assistance with your application, contact the Center at cagr@usc.edu . The Center for Advanced Genocide Research Fellowships are administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Travel grants for current USC students **Marashi Family French Scholarship and Margaret Kershaw Scholarship** The Marashi Family Scholarship is intended to assist students planning to participate in a Maymester program in France (Dijon, Martinique, or Paris). Recipients of the award must be admitted to and commit to one of the following Spring 2027 Maymesters in France: Dijon (FREN 150, 220, 250, or 300); Martinique (FREN 220); or Paris (FREN 307g) Have a declared major or minor in French Have a demonstrated interest in furthering their studies in French Have a strong academic record in their French language course(s) and/or in general Financial need is preferred, but not required The Margaret Kershaw Scholarship for Study in France is intended to further the education of students studying French abroad, with priority given to French majors. FOR SPRING 2027 MAYMESTER STUDENTS: If you would like to be considered for scholarship funding for a Spring 2027 Maymester program in France, please submit the Marashi Family Scholarship application. As the Marashi Family Scholarship is a $2,000 maximum award, you may also submit the Kershaw supplemental scholarship application in addition to the application for the Marashi Family Scholarship if you would like to be considered for funding greater than $2,000. Application deadline for both the Marashi Family French Scholarship and Margaret Kershaw Scholarship: Mon. November 16, 12:00 noon Download the application for the Spring 2027 Marashi Family Scholarship. Download the supplemental application for the Margaret Kershaw Scholarship. FOR SPRING 2027 FULL SEMESTER STUDY ABROAD STUDENTS: If you would like to be considered for scholarship funding for a full semester Spring 2027 study abroad program in France, please submit the version of the Margaret Kershaw Scholarship application below. Do NOT use the Marashi Family Scholarship application or the supplemental Kershaw Scholarship application linked above. Application deadline for the Margaret Kershaw Scholarship for full semester study abroad: Mon. November 16, 12:00 noon Download the application for the Margaret Kershaw Scholarship for students studying abroad in France for a full semester. The Marashi Family Scholarship and the Margaret Kershaw Scholarship are administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Dean Joan Metcalf Schaefer Travel Award The Dean Joan Metcalf Schaefer Travel Award is given to undergraduate students attending the Pembroke Cambridge Summer Programme who have demonstrated academic excellence and a continuous love of learning. Recipients of the Summer 2026 awards must be full-time undergraduate students for the 2026-2027 academic year Junior or senior class standing in the 2026-2027 academic year Minimum 3.5 grade cumulative point average Must be planning to participate in the Pembroke Cambridge Summer Programme in Summer 2026 The application deadline for the Summer 2026 Dean Joan Metcalf Schaefer Travel Award has passed. Please visit this webpage in January 2027 for the application for the Summer 2027 Dean Joan Metcalf Schaefer Travel Award. The Dean Joan Metcalf Schaefer Travel Award is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Del Amo Scholarship for Study Abroad in Spain The Del Amo Scholarship for study abroad in Spain is intended to assist students planning to participate in a semester-long or year-long study abroad program through USC taking place in Spain. The scholarship donor hopes to encourage students to gain a deeper understanding of the language and culture of Spain. Recipients of the Spring 2027 awards must have a declared major in USC Dornsife and federally-determined financial need Minimum 3.0 grade cumulative point average Must be planning to participate in a USC-sponsored study abroad program in Spain in Spring 2027 Spring 2027 Application is Now Closed. Please visit this webpage in February 2027 for the application for the Fall 2027. The Del Amo Scholarship for Study Abroad in Spain is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Ralph and Joan Hovel Memorial Scholarship The Ralph and Joan Hovel Memorial Scholarship is intended to assist students planning to participate in a semester-long or year-long study abroad program through USC taking place in Germany or Austria. The scholarship donor hopes to encourage students to gain a deeper understanding of the language and culture of Germany. Recipients of the Fall 2026 awards must be undergraduate students at USC with a minimum 3.0 cumulative grade point average. Must be planning to participate in a study abroad program in Germany or Austria in Fall 2026 sponsored by the USC Office of Overseas Studies. Must be currently enrolled in a college-level language course in German at USC, or must have previously completed at least one college-level language course in German at USC. Please visit this webpage in February 2027 for the application for the Fall 2027 Ralph and Joan Hovel Memorial Scholarship. The Ralph and Joan Hovel Memorial Scholarship is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Kallins Hellenic Studies Summer Fellowship Established by George and Bettina Kallins. Provides assistance to students for summer study and travel in Greece to explore and discover Greece and its rich cultural heritage. Students may contact the Classics department for information on possible uses of fellowship funds. Current or previous enrollment in at least one course in the Classics department (Classics, Greek, or Latin) 3.0 minimum cumulative GPA Students not currently majoring or minoring in Classics are especially encouraged to apply The application deadline for the 2025 Kallins Hellenic Studies Summer Fellowship has passed. Please check this webpage in January 2026 for information on the Summer 2026 Kallins Hellenic Studies Summer Fellowship. Please visit the Classics department webpage for further information about the fellowship, including a link to the application instructions. The Kallins Hellenic Studies Summer Fellowship is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Gold Family Scholarships for Overseas Studies Believing that overseas experiences have tremendous impact on personal and intellectual growth, Stanley and Ilene Gold established this scholarship for outstanding USC Dornsife Trustee Scholars seeking to study abroad. Must be enrolled (or plan to be enrolled) in a USC-approved study abroad program for Summer 2026, Fall 2026 and/or Spring 2027 USC Trustee Scholarship recipient USC Dornsife major 3.2 minimum cumulative grade point average Junior or senior in the 2026-2027 academic year A recommendation from a USC faculty member is required The application deadline for the 2026-2027 Gold Family Scholarships for Overseas Studies has passed. Please visit this webpage in January 2027 for the application link for the 2027-2028 Gold Family Scholarships for Overseas Studies. Gold Family Scholarships for Overseas Study are administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Scholarships for graduating seniors Beau J. Boudreaux Poetry Award Established in 2005 by Creative Writing alumnus Beau Boudreaux (1994). Given to one graduating senior poet. Graduating senior, eligible to graduate in the 2025-2026 academic year Creative Writing major with an emphasis in Poetry 3.0 cumulative grade point average Preference given to a student who intends to pursue a career in writing or academia and who wishes to supplement his or her undergraduate degree with travel and/or advanced study in a graduate program The application deadline for the 2026 Beau J. Boudreaux Poetry Award has passed. Please visit this webpage in January 2027 for the application for the 2027 Beau J. Boudreaux Poetry Award. The Beau J. Boudreaux Poetry Award is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Gene and Etta Silverman Family Award Established in memory of Gene, Etta, Candace, and Jamie Silverman who died in a plane accident on July 17, 1996. Candace attended USC for four years and majored in Creative Writing. Graduating senior, eligible to graduate in the 2025-2026 academic year Creative Writing major 3.0 cumulative grade point average Preference given to a student who intends to pursue a career in writing or academia, wishing to supplement the undergraduate degree with travel and/or advanced study in a graduate program The application deadline for the 2026 Gene and Etta Silverman Family Award has passed. Please visit this webpage in January 2027 for the application for the 2027 Gene and Etta Silverman Family Award. The Gene and Etta Silverman Family Award is administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Fellowship programs Mellon Mays Undergraduate Fellowships This select group includes sophomores who are considering an academic career and will likely pursue graduate studies in the humanities or social sciences (excluding psychology and economics). USC is one of a select group of colleges and universities who offer this program through the Mellon Foundation. Sophomore standing, but juniors are welcome to apply Pursuing a major or minor in a Mellon-approved field of study Academic promise (cumulative GPA of at least 3.2) Interest in pursuing an academic career in a Mellon-designated field of study Potential for serving as a mentor and teacher for a wide variety of students U.S. citizen or U.S. permanent resident The application deadline for the 2026-2027 cohort has passed. For more information, visit the Mellon Mays Undergraduate Fellowship program website . Mellon Mays Undergraduate Fellowships are administered consistent with the University’s Notice of Non-Discrimination. Recipients are chosen without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, or any other prohibited characteristic. Contact Details Contact us If you have further questions, please contact our office. We’re happy to help! USC Dornsife Admission and Student Success (213) 740-5930 scholarships@dornsife.usc.edu Academics Majors and Minors Undergraduate Education Master’s & Professional Studies PhD Studies Scholarship Award Non-Discrimination Directories & Maps Our Faculty Our Staff Social Media Directory Campus Maps Access & Support Dornsife Technology Services Brand and Identity MyDornsife 365 Web USC Zoom Trojan Family Alumni Relations USC Dornsife Magazine Dornsife Dialogues Commencement Career Pathways Dornsife at a Glance Contact Us Privacy Digital Accessibility Emergency Information Notice of Non-Discrimination Copyright © 2026 University of Southern California	02c28617abbf6d7abdaf5d027ba1192b480