Citation Existing Appeal Status Parking Violations Rules Connect With Us Contact Facebook Feedback FAQ Feedback Contact commuting Commuting & Transit Commuter Cost Calculator USC Employee Transit Subsidy Guaranteed Ride Home USC Student U-Pass Program Metrolink Student Discount Amtrak San Joaquins USC Bike Program Getting to/from LAX USC Student U-Pass Program PLEASE NOTE : You have to have a physical card issued by USC to begin this process; if you don’t have one yet, please visit one of our offices during normal business hours to get one. At UPC: McCarthy Structure (M-F, 8:30-3:00); at HSC: KAM-120 (M-F, 7:30-4:00) Please review this How To Activate your Metro U-Pass Tutorial (7:31 long) for a video walk-through of the process described below. The Fall 2026 program began on August 17, and runs through the beginning of January. The U-Pass program application process is now fully automated; you can begin online by completing this U-Pass form . Once the form is submitted, you will receive a system-generated automatic email confirming your information was received, and directing you to the next steps to complete the registration process. These next steps include: Clicking on the LA Metro link in the confirmation email you receive to visit their activation website Entering your 20 digit card number and the appropriate activation code ( SCFA26 ) Checking the acknowledgement box on LA Metro’s form Clicking the “Submit” button Wait 24 hours, then go tap your card at any station kiosk ( see IMPORTANT NOTE below ) IMPORTANT NOTE : As mentioned in the confirmation email, the final step is that you MUST TAP YOUR ACTIVATED CARD AT ANY LA METRO STATION or BUS WITHIN 14 DAYS TO LOCK IN THE ACTIVATION. Hold it in place for a few seconds until the kiosk beeps. Almost EVERY SINGLE ISSUE related to the U-Pass program is due to students not locking in their activation in a timely manner. ____________________________________________________________ GENERAL PROGRAM INFORMATION USC Transportation partners with LA Metro to offer the U-Pass program every semester — it is available to ALL current USC students (both Graduate students and Undergraduates), and is available at no additional cost (the cost of the program is included in the Transportation Fee being charged to all registered students at USC). The U-Pass allows USC students unlimited rides on LA Metro’s rail and bus lines for any given semester. You DO have to re-register every semester in which you want one, but you DO NOT need a new card every time – if you already have a U-Pass issued by our office from a previous semester, you should choose the “RENEWAL” option during the purchasing process. If you have lost your card , you need to get a new one from our office in the McCarthy Structure at UPC during normal business hours (M-F, 8:30-3:00), and then return to this site to activate it. The U-Pass usually looks like this (below), although other designs may also be used. It is valid for travel on ALL LA Metro bus and rail lines , as well as select partner agencies (see list below). WHERE CAN I USE THE U-PASS? The U-Pass is valid on the following transit agencies: All LA Metro buses and trains. This includes the Metro Rapid bus lines, as well as regular Metro buses including Zones 1. LADOT – DASH buses only. This does not include other LADOT transit services like Commuter Express. Santa Monica Big Blue Bus. If you have issues using your U-Pass on Big Blue Bus please contact their customer service line at (310) 451-5444. Culver City Bus Norwalk Transit System Torrance Transit Gardena Transit Long Beach Transit Foothill Transit (excluding commuter express lines: 490, 493, 495, 498, 499, 699) The pass is not valid on any other services, such as Metrolink, Amtrak, and LAX FlyAway Buses. If you need help or have any questions about the program, please contact USC Transportation’s Rideshare Coordinator via email at tsubsidy@usc.edu . Contact Us USC Transportation 620 McCarthy Way (McCarthy Structure) Los Angeles, CA 90089 T: (213) 740-3575 For office hours or to contact our office at the Health Sciences Campus, click here Quick Links USC Auxiliary Services USC Bookstores USC Hospitality USC Housing USC Hotel Los Angeles Memorial Coliseum and Sports Arena Privacy Policy Digital Accessibility Connect With Us Feedback © Copyright 2026 University of Southern California. All rights reserved. Go to Top Latest USC Transportation Announcements	e068eb3da0299626c0382d52cec1b25ebf1ba257ab528dbe7b9617d215af7461	\N
85	https://uscimmigrationclinic.org/	2026-11-13 18:45:58.153493-08	200	USC Gould School of Law Immigration Clinic Skip to content USC Gould School of Law Immigration Clinic Menu Home Service Projects Immigrant Legal Assistance Center Know Your Rights Events Donate Contact Us URGENT INFORMATION “I would like to … thank you for your kind assistance in making my dream come true and appreciate your professional skill. It’s unforgettable. I will always be grateful and remember it.” -Z.H. (Ethiopia), granted asylum, children granted derivative asylum Learn More About Us The Gould School of Law Immigration Clinic is staffed by law students, faculty, and staff and provides free legal representation to adults and children from all over the world, including Mexico, Central and South America, and Africa. The Immigration Clinic consists of two teaching clinics and a direct service program. Second and third year Gould students enrolled in the Removal Defense Clinic and the Immigration Detention and Appellate Clinic represent clients under the supervision of faculty and staff attorneys. Most of the cases involve applications for asylum, protection under the Convention Against Torture, and other humanitarian forms of relief. Students represent their clients before the Immigration Court, the Board of Immigration Appeals, the U.S. District Court for the Central District of California, and the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit. Many of our clients are detained in ICE detention centers in Southern California and elsewhere. A significant number of our clients are LGBTQ asylum seekers. The Immigration Clinic’s direct service project is the USC Gould Immigrant Legal Assistance Center (ILAC). All legal services provided by the Immigration Clinic are free of chare. Our students and staff speak multiple languages, including Spanish, Farsi, Sinhalese, and conversational Italian, and we rely on volunteer interpreters to supplement our capacity and for other languages when needed. Immigrant Legal Assistance Center The USC Gould Immigrant Legal Assistance Center (ILAC) provides free, confidential consultations, legal assistance, and referrals to members of the USC Trojan Family, students, staff members, contract employees, faculty and family members, who may be at risk of deportation, who may be eligible to apply for immigration status, or who have questions about their legal status and legal rights under the immigration laws. The Legal Assistance Center is supported by the Office of the Provost and USC Gould School of Law. Continue reading “Immigrant Legal Assistance Center” → DACA Renewals ATTENTION USC DACA RECIPIENTS: Although U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services (USCIS) is not accepting initial DACA applications, DACA renewals are currently being processed. The USC Immigrant Legal Assistance Center offers renewal application assistance free of charge. USC First Generation Plus Success Center will pay the renewal filing fee for current USC students. The filing fees increased on April 1, 2024. The filing fee for the Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals (I-821D) application is $85. The filing fee for the Employment Authorization Document(I-765) is $470 if filed online and $520 if filed by mail. The total filing fee is therefore $555 or $605. We recommend that anyone with DACA seek legal consultation regardless of when your DACA status expires. Contact our office to schedule a free and confidential consultation with our staff attorneys by emailing iclinic@law.usc.edu , calling 213-821-9627, or scheduling an appointment at https://calendly.com/usc-ilac . Continue reading “DACA Renewals” → Transforming Lives. Reuniting Families. Current Gould Students Are you a current USC Gould student or are you interested in applying to USC Gould School of Law? Visit Gould’s website to learn how to enroll in the Removal Continue reading Current Gould Students → Volunteer with us Interested in volunteering for our office? Whether you are a current student, immigration attorney, or community member, you may sign up to volunteer. Email us at iclinic@law.usc.edu for more information. Testimonials “I would like to thank you and your staff so much for all the hard work in helping me obtain my U.S. Citizenship. I know the process would have been Continue reading Testimonials → contact us Office: Mailing Address: Immigration Clinic 699 Exposition Blvd, Los Angeles, CA 90089-0071 Follow Us Facebook X Instagram Hours & Info 213-821-5987 iclinic@law.usc.edu Monday - Friday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm Citizenship Project 213-821-9627 Immigrant Legal Assistance Center 213-821-9627 Translate	ad00755654d7b0aa86243d8bdb4018d74ebc20d9d1f342a83e819330bd10f5c1	\N
